/// Limit x to a range.
///
/// The value of `x` brought into the range from `low` to `high`: `low` where `x` is below it,
/// `high` where `x` is above it, and `x` itself in between. It is `min(max(x, low), high)`, so an
/// Int stays an Int when all three are Ints, `NA` in any argument gives `NA`, and when `low` is
/// above `high` the result is `high`, as R's `pmin(pmax(x, low), high)` gives. Given a column, it
/// applies to every row in one pass.
///
/// x: The value, or a column of values.
/// low: The smallest value the result may have.
/// high: The largest value the result may have.
/// returns: `x` within the range, a Float, or an Int when all three are Ints; a column when `x` is one.
/// details: Exact: the result is one of its three arguments, and nothing is rounded.
/// reference: Equivalent to R's `pmin(pmax(x, low), high)`; Stata has no single function (`min(max(x, low), high)`).
/// see also: min, max, between
/// > clamp(5, 0, 3)
/// 3
/// > clamp(-1.5, 0, 3)
/// 0
/// > clamp(1.5, 0, 3)
/// 1.5
/// > let d = frame(x = [-2, 0.5, 7])
/// > clamp(d["x"], 0, 3)
/// Column<Float> · 3 values
/// 0
/// 0.5
/// 3
fn clamp(x: Float, low: Float, high: Float) -> Float {
    min(max(x, low), high)
}
